WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.179
Examples are the strongest lever you have, so aim them.

2
00:00:03.459 --> 00:00:04.781
Showing beats telling.

3
00:00:05.061 --> 00:00:16.880
A few examples of input and desired output (called few-shot or multishot prompting) are, in Anthropic’s words, one of the most reliable ways to steer format, tone, and structure.

4
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:28.637
Google’s Gemini guide goes further and recommends including few-shot examples in prompts as a default, noting that clear examples can sometimes replace instructions entirely.

5
00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:31.861
Anthropic’s docs give three properties to aim for.

6
00:00:32.141 --> 00:00:36.792
Examples should be relevant, mirroring your real inputs rather than toy cases.

7
00:00:37.072 --> 00:00:43.664
They should be diverse, covering edge cases and varying enough that the model doesn’t latch onto an accidental pattern.

8
00:00:43.944 --> 00:00:51.688
And they should be structured, wrapped in <example> tags (inside an <examples> block) so the model can tell them apart from instructions.

9
00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:55.232
The docs suggest three to five examples as a starting point.

10
00:00:55.512 --> 00:01:04.216
Google adds two cautions: keep the format identical across examples, and don’t include so many that the model starts copying them too closely.

11
00:01:04.496 --> 00:01:10.000
For a PM-flavored case, say you want status updates labeled on_track, at_risk, or blocked.

12
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:19.987
Good examples would include a short update and a rambling one, one where the risk is buried in the last sentence, and one that sounds alarming but is actually on track.

13
00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:24.149
Each label appears at least once, and no single label dominates.
